  "narrative_body": "France assembled roughly 50 nations in Paris this week to plan a naval mission that Washington was explicitly not invited to join.\n\nThe April 17 conference, co-chaired by Macron and Keir Starmer, was aimed at securing merchant shipping through the Strait of Hormuz. The Élysée made the logic plain: France \"will not enter a coalition with the Americans simply because we are not parties to the conflict.\" Macron described the initiative as a \"third way\" between American maximum pressure on Iran and an Iranian blockade. France and Britain will co-lead the military planning, with a follow-on conference in London. Dozens of countries have offered assets. The mission is conditional on the ceasefire holding, so no ships sail imminently — but the institutional architecture now exists, and 50 governments signed on to it.\n\nNot everyone was comfortable with Washington's exclusion. Friedrich Merz, the German chancellor, said publicly it would be \"desirable\" for the US to be involved — a dissent that reveals France's framing as contested even among its closest partners. The coalition's durability will depend partly on whether those partners accept the non-American premise once military planning turns concrete.\n\nThe same week, a French soldier died in southern Lebanon. Sgt-Chef Florian Montorio of the 17th Parachute Engineer Regiment was killed on April 18 in a roadside ambush near Ghanduriyah while leading a FINUL mine-clearance patrol to restore communications to an isolated post cut off for several days by fighting. He was hit by small arms fire at close range and could not be revived; three comrades were wounded, two seriously. Catherine Vautrin, the armed forces minister, confirmed the operational details. France's roughly 700 troops in Lebanon are not sitting in static positions — they are conducting live mine-clearance missions in hostile conditions during a fragile ceasefire.\n\nMacron moved fast. He publicly attributed responsibility to Hezbollah — more assertive than France's usual hedged language in FINUL incidents — and demanded \"immediate arrests\" from Lebanese authorities. He announced he would receive Nawaf Salam, the Lebanese prime minister, at the Élysée on April 21, converting a military casualty into diplomatic pressure. Hezbollah denied involvement; FINUL's initial findings pointed to \"non-state actors.\" A further complication: the American president publicly accused Iran of targeting a French vessel in the strait — a claim the Élysée had not confirmed from available reporting, and one that, if true, would fundamentally alter the \"non-belligerent\" premise of the Hormuz initiative. The claim warrants watching.\n\nThe contrast between France's external confidence and its domestic condition could hardly be sharper. At home, Sébastien Lecornu, the prime minister, suffered what French outlets called his \"black week\" — the sharpest parliamentary deterioration since he took office in October. In a single week, the unemployment insurance bill was defeated not by opposition votes but by his own coalition's absenteeism, which Les Echos called a \"major setback.\" He retreated on May 1st work legislation after trade unions threatened censure motions, allowing florists and bakers to open without sanction rather than risk a vote. The antisemitism bill was withdrawn. Lecornu warned publicly of \"worrying demobilisation\" among deputies; privately, coalition members were rather more direct, telling reporters he had made them \"look like fools.\" Le Parisien reported that Lecornu himself \"didn't expect it to go up in flames\" — suggesting he lacks reliable intelligence about his own parliamentary bloc.\n\nThe rift with Gabriel Attal — his predecessor as prime minister and a rival for 2027 — is now in the open. Attal boycotted the Monday coalition leaders' dinner at Matignon. Lecornu's approval fell 4 points to 32%, its lowest since he took office; Macron's also fell, with the fuel price crisis feeding discontent with a government simultaneously promising a long-term electrification transformation. That transformation faces its own pressure: a Marianne cover story called the programme a \"grande illusion\" — opinion rather than reporting, but it reflects a wider elite scepticism about whether the 10 billion euro annual commitment can be delivered by a government that cannot pass its own unemployment insurance bill.\n\nWhile Lecornu struggles, Jordan Bardella is building. On April 20, the National Rally president lunched with all 15 members of Medef's executive bureau — a first for his party — arriving with a new economic adviser, François Durvye, and a joint letter from himself and Marine Le Pen asking business leaders to list regulatory obstacles ahead of a proposed simplification decree ready for publication if the RN wins in 2027. Mr Bardella told the room \"this is not an audition,\" inverting the usual dynamic. Patrick Martin, Medef's president, drew the conclusion plainly: \"We will have to talk to representatives of the National Rally.\" Business opinion remains divided — executives at Le Pen's earlier dinner at Drouant grew tense over her euroscepticism and plans to reverse pension reform — but the shift from private dinners to a formal meeting with Medef's executive bureau is qualitative. Marylise Léon of the CFDT called the engagement \"cynicism.\" Whether business elites want it or not, the RN is now treated as a government-in-waiting by France's main employer federation.",
  "other_stories": [
    {
      "headline": "French FINUL soldier killed in Lebanon ambush, Macron blames Hezbollah and summons Lebanese PM",
      "summary": "Sergeant-chef Florian Montorio of the 17th Parachute Engineer Regiment was killed on April 18 in a roadside ambush in southern Lebanon while on a FINUL mission, with three other French soldiers wounded. President Macron publicly attributed responsibility to Hezbollah, demanded immediate arrests from Lebanese authorities, and announced plans to receive Lebanese Prime Minister Nawaf Salam at the Élysée on April 21; Montorio's remains were repatriated to France on April 19."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Lecornu government hit by week of parliamentary defeats, popularity falls to record lows",
      "summary": "In what outlets described as a 'black week', Prime Minister Lecornu faced the rejection of an unemployment insurance bill by his own coalition's absenteeism, retreated on May 1st work legislation after syndicates threatened censure, watched an antisemitism bill withdrawn, and saw his approval rating fall four points to 32% — its lowest since his appointment in October 2025. The episode exposed deepening tensions with his predecessor Gabriel Attal, who is positioning for the 2027 presidential race."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bardella-princess romance goes public in Paris Match, triggering Le Monde lawsuit threat and media storm",
      "summary": "RN president Jordan Bardella officially confirmed his relationship with aristocrat Maria Carolina de Bourbon des Deux-Siciles during the France 2 evening news on April 15 after Paris Match published photographs taken in Corsica; he subsequently threatened to sue Le Monde after the newspaper published corrections following inaccuracies about a private jet. The episode generated extensive coverage about its impact on the RN's political positioning ahead of 2027."
    }
  ]
}

